      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**We spent five months building a feature that almost nobody used.**\n\nNot \"slow adoption.\" Not \"mixed reception.\" After 90 days, you could fit our weekly active users into one elevator.\n\nHere's how it happened.\n\nReporting was the #1 request in every sales call. Prospects saw competitors' customizable dashboards and asked why we didn't have one. So we did the obvious thing: we built the best dashboard we could imagine. 20+ widgets. Drag-and-drop layouts. Saved views. Color-coded everything.\n\nThe internal demo got applause. The launch post got hundreds of likes.\n\nThe adoption graph was a flatline with a small bump on launch day. 1.4% of accounts had ever even opened it.\n\nSo we finally did what we should've done five months earlier: we got on calls with the exact customers who asked for it.\n\nOne of them, an ops manager, said something I still think about:\n\n\"Oh, I saw it. It's beautiful. I just don't have time to build a dashboard.\"\n\nThen she told us what she actually did every Friday: export raw data into a spreadsheet, clean it up, and email a summary to her boss. Every. Single. Week.\n\nShe didn't want a dashboard. She wanted that hour of her life back.\n\nSix weeks later, we shipped the least glamorous feature I've ever been part of: a button that said \"Email me this report every Friday.\"\n\nIt became our most-adopted feature that year.\n\nThe lesson I keep coming back to:\n\n**Users describe solutions, not problems.**\n\nWhen someone says \"we need a dashboard,\" they're handing you a spec for the solution they can imagine — not the problem they actually have. Our job is to ask \"why?\" until we find the job underneath.\n\nFive months of engineering, or two weeks of watching customers work. Both would've found the Friday email. Only one of them cost us half a roadmap.\n\nNow every feature request gets one question before anything else: \"Walk me through what you were doing right before you decided you needed this.\"\n\nThe answers are almost never what the request said. They're always better.\n\nWhat's the most expensive \"nobody used it\" lesson you've ever shipped?\n\n#productmanagement #lessonslearned #buildwhatmatters\n\n---\n\n*Happy to swap in your actual feature, numbers, and industry if you'd like a version tailored to your real story.*\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nWe spent 4 months building a feature that 11 people used.\n\nNot 11%. Eleven humans.\n\nThe feature was a custom report builder for our analytics product. Customers had asked for it — in surveys, in support tickets, on sales calls. \"We need more flexible reporting.\" So we listened. Or we thought we did.\n\nWe scoped it. Designed it. Built it. Polished it. Shipped it with a launch email and an in-app tour.\n\nCrickets.\n\nFor weeks I checked the usage dashboard every morning, half-hoping the data was wrong. It wasn't.\n\nHere's what actually went wrong — and it took me an embarrassingly long time to see it:\n\nWe heard the words \"we want flexible reporting\" and built *our interpretation* of that sentence. We never asked what people were trying to do when they said it.\n\nTurns out, customers didn't want to build reports. They wanted an answer to one specific question: \"Which campaigns drove revenue last month?\" Ideally in one click. Better yet, pushed to them every Monday morning.\n\nWe built a workshop. They wanted a vending machine.\n\nThe lesson that stuck with me:\n\n👉 Customers describe solutions. Your job is to excavate the problem underneath.\n\nWhen someone requests a feature, the most valuable follow-up isn't \"what should it do?\" It's \"walk me through the last time you needed this.\"\n\nThat single question has saved my team months of wasted work since. Sometimes it leads to building something smaller. Sometimes it means building nothing at all — because the real pain lives somewhere else entirely.\n\nThe epilogue: we eventually shipped a simple \"Top campaigns by revenue\" card on the main dashboard. It took three days to build. It's now one of the most-viewed elements in the product.\n\nThree days of work, aimed at the actual problem, beat four months of work aimed at the stated request.\n\nI still think about those 11 users sometimes. (Thank you for trying it. You deserved better.)\n\nIf you're about to kick off a big build, try this before writing a single line of code: ask five customers to describe the last time they felt the pain you're solving. Watch what they *do*, not just what they say.\n\nYou'll either build it better — or realize you should build something else entirely.\n\nWhat's a feature you shipped that flopped? And what did the flop teach you?\n\n#ProductManagement #SoftwareDevelopment #BuildInPublic